: "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes — **that’s the part that makes your frustration reasonable**. I pulled the prior thread back up. Tony’s email was essentially: *“If I recall, didn’t you tell ATEX you were going to be in town on these dates? … they may give some pushback if they thought you were already going to be there.”* The problem is that your original representation was **Florida**, not “I’ll already be sitting in Orlando and therefore the company has no travel obligation.” memcite\n\nAnd more importantly, the original recruiting premise included **“the company will help Don relocate.”** That doesn’t automatically mean “every flight is guaranteed reimbursable,” because the relocation structure apparently was not yet reduced to a written offer. But that is exactly why this is a recruiter issue to resolve, not something Tony should be batting back to you as though you created an unreasonable expense. memcite\n\nFrom a recruiting/process standpoint, his job here is to pin down three things with ATEX:\n\n- what **“relocation assistance”** actually means,\n- whether **interview / transition travel** is covered separately,\n- and whether they expect you to **front costs** or whether they book/pay directly.\n\nThe dollar amount makes the whole thing look even sillier. We’re not talking about a $1,500 last-minute business-class itinerary. We just found viable Florida positioning in the **$150–$200 airfare range**. If the employer genuinely intends to relocate a senior hire, arguing over whether a roughly $200 one-way flight qualifies should be a five-minute clarification between recruiter and client.\n\nSo I would frame the underlying issue exactly this way: **you’re not demanding that Tony personally approve an expense. You’re expecting the recruiter who sold the opportunity partly on relocation assistance to go back to the client, define the relocation package, and give you a clean answer.**\n\nThat’s the miss