ntroductions and then let him operate.\n\nCritical early introductions:\n\nconsulting group → IT group → software/integration vendors → department heads → operational leadership.\n\nRelationships start immediately even if the actual work waits 30–60 days.\n\nFriday outbound agenda\n\nAn agenda has already been drafted in the A-Tex artifact.\n\nIt positions the meeting around:\n\ncurrent environment → pain/risk/priorities → current ownership → roadmap/resources → definition of success.\n\nNo compensation, relocation, admin-hiring theory, or giant prebuilt solution belongs in that email.\n\nThe agenda should give John something substantive to ingest before Friday without giving him homework.\n\n30 / 60 / 90 model\n\nKeep this at executive altitude.\n\n30 days — Onboard / Ground\n\nLearn the company before redesigning anything.\n\nMeet leadership and department heads. Understand Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales, NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, integrations, reporting, vendors, consultants, IT, current projects, and pain points.\n\nRequest introductions immediately.\n\nBuild the current-state map.\n\nAsk every department leader:\n\n“Give me the 5–10 systems or process problems that frustrate you most, slow the team down, create risk, or stop you from getting information you need.”\n\nDo not promise to solve everything.\n\n60 days — Stable / Quick Wins\n\nPeter now understands the environment.\n\nPrioritize the pain inventory by:\n\nbusiness impact → urgency → dependency → effort → risk → correct owner.\n\nThen route each issue:\n\nPeter solves it → quick proof of concept → internal resource → consultant/vendor → roadmap → deprioritize.\n\nAttack high-impact / low-effort wins.\n\nEstablish two cadences:\n\nExecutive Priority Review with John / sponsor.\n\nDelivery Review with department/resource owners.\n\nStart removing recurring systems burden from John and other leaders.\n\n90 days — Roadmap Execution\n\nPeter is no longer onboarding.\n\nBegin larger roadmap execution.\